# boundary — which repository owns each path, and why.
#
# THE FAILURE THIS PREVENTS is a half-migration. The cockpit's shell tools were pointed at
# one database while the panel still wrote to another; within the hour a conversation was
# split across two stores and an answer reached nobody. A boundary written on only one side
# of itself drifts exactly that way, so this file is the single source and `boundary.sh`
# renders it into every document that publishes it — this repository's README, and the
# operator's own wiki page when one is configured.
#
# Format:  owner | path | what it is
#
#   spira    ships in the harness repo. Generic mechanism, carrying no operator data.
#   brain    stays in the operator's wiki repo, if they keep one. Everything whose write
#            target is a page. Named for the repository this harness was extracted from.
#   neither  data. It belongs to whoever runs the harness and is in no shared repo.
#
# TWO RULES DECIDE EVERY CASE, INCLUDING THE ONES NOT LISTED BELOW:
#
#   1. THE WRITE TARGET DECIDES OWNERSHIP. A program belongs to the repository whose files
#      it writes, whatever it reads. Reading across the boundary is free; writing across it
#      is what makes ownership arguable, and every genuinely ambiguous path below is a
#      program that reads one side and writes the other.
#   2. DEPENDENCY RUNS ONE WAY: wiki -> harness, never back. The harness must start from a
#      clean clone with no wiki present anywhere on the machine. Where it wants an effect on
#      the wiki it calls a CONFIGURED hook and carries on when that hook is absent — an
#      optional call is not a dependency, a hard path is.
#
# THE ROWS DESCRIBE WHERE A PATH BELONGS, and in this repository that is also where it is.
# While the harness was being extracted the renderer derived a "still to move" list from the
# gap between the two; it is gone because the gap is closed, and a migration aid left standing
# after its migration reads as a plan rather than a record.

# ---- the harness: generic mechanism, no operator data ----------------------------------
spira   | spira/                        | the harness proper — aeon runner, sentinel and its checks, gate, sending, strand, drain, the chamber and its fayth format, lib.sh, and the test suites that hold them
spira   | spira/boundary                | this manifest — it describes the harness, so it travels with it
spira   | spira/boundary.sh             | renders this manifest into every document that publishes it; the wiki-side target is configured and skipped when unset, per rule 2
spira   | spira/conf.sh                 | the one configuration surface: the loader, the key allowlist, the derived defaults, and `spira_require`, which names a missing program instead of dying as a shell error
spira   | spira/repo-map.example        | example rows showing the six columns. The real rows are operator data
spira   | spira/exclude.sh              | keeps the beads database and its exports out of this repository — the check the gate runs, the pre-commit hook, and the installer that arms both. A beads database is never public
spira   | spira/hooks/                  | the pre-commit hook itself, TRACKED and armed by core.hooksPath. .git/hooks is not cloned, so a hook that lived there would reach a colleague missing and unannounced
spira   | spira/inventory.sh            | the fence that keeps one operator's infrastructure out of a repository meant to be cloned — repository names, hosts, paths, people, dates. It scans comments, which is where all of it was
spira   | spira/inventory-deny          | the tokens that fence refuses beyond the structural ones. Ships EMPTY: a list of somebody else's names is itself the inventory
spira   | spira/actors.example          | commit author to harness, for authors the commit graph cannot vote on. Its rows are one installation's roster
spira   | spira/auron.sh                | the watchdog over the loop — reads timestamps and counters, raises or clears an alert bead. Its only power is speech: it repairs nothing, restarts nothing and summons nothing
spira   | spira/skew.sh                 | is the activated release the latest published — the hourly check that the installed release matches the most recent release tag; also the landing gate's fence against work landing in a copy nothing executes
spira   | spira/doctor.sh               | read-only preflight — every missing program, unreadable database, unmapped repository and unbuilt panel, named in one pass
spira   | spira/incident.sh             | turns a production event into a bead Ops can claim — systemd OnFailure, arbitrary payload, or a spool drain; deduplicates by external_ref
spira   | spira/statutes/               | the SEED statute book, one file per statute. Statutes live in the beads KV store, which is per-installation, so a clone gets the mechanism and none of the law unless it ships as text
spira   | spira/seed.sh                 | writes those statutes into a fresh database, and never over one already in force
spira   | cockpit/                      | the decisions panel (Rust) and the ops pane — how a human sees what the harness is doing and answers what it asks. Generic; it reads whatever database it is pointed at
spira   | loom/                         | Loom — a Rust read endpoint over the live beads graph with a per-request budget; refuses to start without a database so it cannot silently serve another harness's graph
spira   | systemd/                      | unit TEMPLATES plus install.sh. The units in force on a machine are generated from these, never edited in place
spira   | install.sh                    | the one entry point: nine sequential phases — preflight, conflict checks, config, build, database, units, hooks, cockpit, verify — ending with ready.sh
spira   | spira/uninstall.sh            | inverse of install.sh; walks owned.sh so the two cannot drift; instance-aware and refuses to guess when multiple instances are installed
spira   | spira/owned.sh                | single declaration of what one installation owns outside the checkout — the load-bearing contract walked by both installer and uninstaller
spira   | spira/ready.sh                | postflight: seven readiness checks after install; exit 3 when installed-but-not-ready
spira   | spira/build.sh                | builds Loom and the cockpit panel; a clone that skips this gets a service that exits 2 and an empty panel
spira   | spira/configure.sh            | bootstraps ~/.config/spira/ on first install; never overwrites an existing file
spira   | spira/testenv.sh              | rootless podman container with user systemd for the install-rehearsal suite tier
spira   | docs/                         | spike investigations and evidence files from the harness's own development
spira   | concierge.sh                  | one named Remote Control session, so a phone can reach the harness
spira   | rule.sh                       | enacting a statute writes the beads KV store, which is the harness's substrate
spira   | spira/archive.sh              | keeps every session transcript and indexes it by time range and by the lineage id that survives a clear. The mechanism ships; the transcripts and the store they land in are the operator's own and stay out of every repository
spira   | beads-push.sh                 | pushes the beads database to its configured Dolt remote. The mechanism ships; the remote it is pointed at is the operator's own and is private
spira   | spira.conf.example            | the annotated template an operator copies to spira.conf. Every key optional, every default derived from where the harness is installed
spira   | README.md                     | the harness's own entry point, carrying this table
spira   | AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md          | how an agent works ON the harness. Distinct from the wiki repository's own agent instructions, which are how an overseer works WITH it

# ---- the wiki: everything whose write target is a page ----------------------------------
# Paths in the operator's own notes repository, if they keep one. They are here because the
# rule that places them is the harness's — the write target decides ownership — not because
# the harness knows or needs anything about that repository.
brain   | wiki/                         | the operator's knowledge base: pages, journal, projects, notes
brain   | raw/                          | source documents, and any JSONL mirror of the database kept in a PRIVATE repository
brain   | the wiki's own agent instructions | its catalog, its chronological log, and how an overseer works with the harness
brain   | a tasks generator             | regenerates a page listing the beads that need the operator
brain   | a statute-book generator      | regenerates the readable copy of the statutes in force
brain   | its cron wrapper              | runs that generator on a timer and commits only on substantive change
brain   | a beads exporter              | writes the JSONL mirror into a private repository
brain   | a bead-table generator        | regenerates a plan table on a wiki page from the bead graph
brain   | that repository's own gate    | one repository's landing gate, named by its row in repo-map. Every repository declares its own
brain   | a checkbox ticker             | ticks a checkbox on the wiki page that holds it — the one cockpit tool that writes a page
brain   | its session guards            | PreToolUse fences for the overseer's own session. A guard binds the actor who broke the rule, so they live with that actor
brain   | its session settings          | that session's own hooks and environment
brain   | its skills                    | whatever lands its output in the wiki

# ---- data: in neither repository --------------------------------------------------------
neither | the beads database            | served by Dolt, addressed as a path. It accumulates internal working notes and agent memories, so it is never public and never in a shared repo
neither | the statutes in force         | rows in that database's KV store, per-installation. A wiki may render a read-only copy; the harness ships SEED statute text an installer writes into a fresh database
neither | spira.conf, repo-map          | the operator's real paths, repositories and personas. The examples ship; these do not. Both are gitignored, and spira.conf is looked for outside the checkout first for that reason
neither | the systemd units in force    | rendered from systemd/ templates by install.sh, filled from spira.conf. Never edited in place — `systemd/install.sh --diff` is how you find out somebody did
neither | the transcript archive        | compressed session logs plus their index, written by archive.sh. They carry paths, credentials read aloud and everything anyone ever said, so they live outside every checkout and no shared repository holds them. Nothing deletes them: retention is the operator's decision
neither | .runtime/                     | logs, worktrees, leases, cockpit state. Regenerated, machine-local, gitignored
